edev-marigold: Hey folks — onboarding hit a weird wall. When we ramped embeddings from our serverless workers we see intermittent 401s and bursts of 429s, mainly around batch jobs. Happening within a minute of deploy. Anyone seen this pattern?

sam-cs: Thanks for flagging, sorry about the friction. Do you have the SDK init snippet and a small sample of logs? 401 + 429 together usually points to token/rotation + fanout. :thinking_face:

edev-marigold: Snippet from our Netlify function:
```js
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_KEY, keepAlive: true, batchSize: 512, proxy: 'https://proxy.internal' });
await client.embeddings.create({ input: docs });
```
Logs: "embed request failed 401 unauthorized", then immediately a flood: "429 rate limit exceeded - embed_units". No obvious clock drift.

alex-eng: Two quick suspects: 1) serverless cold-starts + keepAlive + proxy connection pooling causing many parallel preflights, 2) ephemeral token rotation mismatch (multiple warm instances requesting new short-lived tokens). Both can create auth storms then RL.

edev-marigold: We do rotate tokens from our auth service every 60s and each Lambda instance fetches a fresh token on cold start. Could that be it?

sam-cs: Yes — if N instances cold start and each calls your token service, then they all hit Redwood almost simultaneously with new tokens; if token TTLs overlap or proxy caches stale header, you'll see 401 then 429 as retries pile up.

alex-eng: Quick mitigations to try now: 1) Reduce batchSize to 64-128 for serverless to lower per-request cost and reduce unit spike. 2) Add a small randomized jitter to token refresh (splay 0-30s). 3) Prefer process-local token cache with short lease and a single refresh lock. 4) If using an HTTP proxy, ensure it forwards Authorization header and doesn't cache responses.

edev-marigold: Trying reduced batchSize now and adding jitter. Also saw a header we add: `x-service-token` — could that cause auth mismatch?

marta-support: If the proxy rewrites or strips Authorization or inserts custom auth headers, our gateway may reject with 401. Also some proxies respond 200 to health checks but fail auth on actual embed endpoints. Can you share proxy config?

edev-marigold: proxy is nginx w/ auth_request to internal auth service, and we forward headers: `proxy_set_header Authorization $http_authorization; proxy_set_header X-Service-Token $http_x_service_token;`. No caching configured.

alex-eng: That looks fine but check for `auth_request_set` behavior — if the auth subrequest returns 200 with a different auth header expectation it can cause a mismatch. Also look for multiple parallel keep-alive sockets from the serverless pool — those multiply the number of outgoing requests during warmup.

sam-cs: Temporary workaround: add server-side concurrency limit (queueing) on your warmup job so only 2-4 parallel embedding requests at a time, and enable exponential backoff with randomized base for retries. We'll also temporarily raise the per-customer embed unit window while you complete testing.

edev-marigold: After reducing batch -> 128 and limiting parallelism to 4, 401s dropped to near zero and 429s smoothed. Looks promising. Thanks! :party_parrot:

marta-support: Great — we'll follow up with an enablement session to walk through token lease patterns and serverless best-practices. Would Monday at 10am PT work?

edev-marigold: Works for us. Could you also share a short example of a process-local token cache pattern?

alex-eng: Sending a tiny pattern to copy: 
```js
// single-refresh lock, lease cached per process
let token = null; let leaseExp = 0; let refreshing = false;
async function getToken() {
  const now = Date.now()/1000;
  if (token && now < leaseExp - 10) return token;
  if (refreshing) { await sleep(100 + Math.random()*200); return token; }
  refreshing = true;
  try { const res = await fetch('/token-service'); token = await res.text(); leaseExp = now + 55 + Math.random()*25; return token; } finally { refreshing = false; }
}
```

sam-cs: I'll open a short enablement doc and record the session. Also tagging deploy-bot for traces.
deploy-bot: Deployment 2026-03-22T16:18:02Z — rollout completed to netlify-prod for marigold.

edev-marigold: Appreciated — will run a longer soak and report back. Thanks everyone! :thumbsup:
